{"name":"Texas Sage","hex":"#b9a77c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"texas","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The topmost cabin deck on a steamboat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/texas"},{"term":"sage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Wise.","Grave; serious; solemn."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very wise person or spiritual teacher; someone of gravity and wisdom, especially, a teacher venerable for years, and of sound judgment and prudence; a grave or stoic philosopher."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sage"}],"entries":[{"title":"Texas sage","blocks":[{"text":"Texas sage may refer to:"},{"items":["Leucophyllum frutescens, an evergreen shrub in the figwort family, native to Texas, that is not a true sage","Salvia coccinea, blood sage","Salvia texana, a Salvia that is also native to Texas"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_sage"},{"title":"Texas","blocks":[{"text":"Texas is a state in the United States. Located in the south-central part of the country, it borders Louisiana, Arkansas, Oklahoma, and New Mexico. It also borders Mexico along the Rio Grande, and has a coastline on the Gulf of Mexico. Covering 268,596 square miles (695,660 km2) and with an estimated population of over 31.7 million residents in 2025, it is the second-largest state by both area and population. Texas is nicknamed the \"Lone Star State\" for the single star depicted on the Republic of Texas flag in 1836 during the Texas war for independence from Mexico, symbolizing Texans' enduring independent spirit and distinct national identity. The lone star design was originally flown in 1819 during the Long Expedition, an early attempt to liberate Texas from Spanish imperial rule."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Flag_of_Texas.svg/330px-Flag_of_Texas.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas"},{"title":"Sage (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Sage is a grey-green resembling that of dried sage leaves. As a quaternary color, it is an equal mix of the tertiary colors citron and slate. The hex RGB color value of the adjacent sage swatch is BCB88A. For decades, some military flight jackets were made in sage green."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Sage_iPhone_17.jpg/330px-Sage_iPhone_17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/2004_TX_Proof.png/500px-2004_TX_Proof.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/2004_TX_Proof.png/500px-2004_TX_Proof.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2004_TX_Proof.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Early_indian_west.jpg/500px-Early_indian_west.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Early_indian_west.jpg/1280px-Early_indian_west.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Early_indian_west.jpg/500px-Early_indian_west.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early Native American tribal territories","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarly_indian_west.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/LaFora_1771_2.jpg/500px-LaFora_1771_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/LaFora_1771_2.jpg/500px-LaFora_1771_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.